            </encodings>
            <customized-tooltip>
              <formatted-text>
                <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>SUVs Dominate Claims</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>: SUVs have the highest claim amounts for both old and new claims, with $8.32M and $3.52M, respectively. Pickups follow with $4.81M (old) and $1.98M (new), while Panel Trucks record the lowest claims at $1.58M and $0.87M.</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>Top Claim-Makers by Gender</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>: Women lead in old claims for SUVs ($8.86M) and sports cars ($4.79M), while men dominate minivans ($5.23M) and pickups ($3.69M). For new claims, women lead SUV and sports car accidents ($3.52M and $1.66M), while men top pickups ($1.57M) and minivans ($1.3M). Women remain the largest claimant group overall.</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>Education vs. Income</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>: Drivers with a PhD boast the highest average income at $0.12M, followed by those with a Master&apos;s at $0.08M. The lowest average income, $0.03M, belongs to drivers with less than a high school education.</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>Frequent Claimants Lead New Claims</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='9'>: Drivers with 5 previous claims file the highest old and new claims. Among new claimants, those with a single prior claim report the highest new claim amounts.</run>
              </formatted-text>
            </customized-tooltip>
            <style>
